1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,093
This is Whodunit. Everyone can show you
the difference between two sets of traces.

2
00:00:06,173 --> 00:00:08,242
Only SigNoz can arm it.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:16,093
Root cause at three a.m. is a human
grinding filter permutations in a query

4
00:00:16,173 --> 00:00:21,139
builder. BubbleUp, Trace Patterns, compare
— they all show you the difference.

5
00:00:21,219 --> 00:00:23,701
Then you type the query yourself.

6
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,852
Whodunit asks ClickHouse one question,
then ranks seven thousand eight hundred

7
00:00:29,932 --> 00:00:34,483
candidate patterns locally in Python —
polars, hand-rolled FP-growth — and

8
00:00:34,563 --> 00:00:38,288
compiles the winner into a real SigNoz
trace-operator query.

9
00:00:38,368 --> 00:00:39,609
No model involved.

10
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:46,266
It leans on five surfaces. One
clickhouse-sql scan joins traces and logs

11
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:51,311
in the same database. The finding becomes
a query-builder expression, a Trace

12
00:00:51,391 --> 00:00:55,529
Explorer link, a Perses v6 panel, and an
armed alert.

13
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:01,024
Six seeded faults, run live: six out of
six.

14
00:01:01,104 --> 00:01:06,897
Three discriminators found at full recall,
and three where the only honest answer was

15
00:01:06,977 --> 00:01:08,177
to abstain.

16
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:13,852
SigNoz's co-founder opened this issue
three and a half years ago.

17
00:01:13,932 --> 00:01:17,656
Compare two sets of filtered spans. It's
still open.

18
00:01:17,736 --> 00:01:18,977
Here's the implementation.

19
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,438
One question to ClickHouse. Seven thousand
eight hundred candidate itemsets

20
00:01:25,518 --> 00:01:27,587
enumerated locally, across thirty-six
features.

21
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:32,219
Watch what the machine rejects. The
payment-to-redis-retry edge on its own:

22
00:01:32,299 --> 00:01:37,264
one point four times lift — it's in the
healthy traces too.

23
00:01:37,344 --> 00:01:40,654
Missing flag-service on its own: one point
nine.

24
00:01:40,734 --> 00:01:46,941
Also in both. Only the conjunction — that
edge and no flag-service — separates the

25
00:01:47,021 --> 00:01:51,573
cohorts, at nine times lift. Eighty-nine
bad traces, zero healthy ones.

26
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:59,266
Same matrix, flat baseline — a proper
BubbleUp-style z-test over every single

27
00:01:59,346 --> 00:02:03,483
feature. Its top pick is precision zero
point two three.

28
00:02:03,563 --> 00:02:09,357
The fault needs two conditions at once,
and no single predicate can see it.

29
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,852
No model wrote this. It's compiled into
SigNoz's own trace-operator grammar:

30
00:02:18,932 --> 00:02:25,552
leaves A, B and C, the expression A
implies B and not C, returning spans from

31
00:02:25,632 --> 00:02:31,012
A. Then Whodunit runs that query back
against the live engine as a

32
00:02:31,092 --> 00:02:33,161
count-distinct. It mined eighty-nine
traces.

33
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:38,620
SigNoz returned eighty-nine. They agree —
and if they hadn't, you'd see the

34
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,355
mismatch, not a paragraph.

35
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:49,679
That permalink opens the machine-written
query in SigNoz's own Trace Explorer — all

36
00:02:49,759 --> 00:02:53,897
three leaves and the operator, rendered in
the real UI.

37
00:02:53,977 --> 00:02:57,288
The result set collapses to the matched
traces.

38
00:02:57,368 --> 00:03:03,161
Open one, and the flame graph shows the
shape: the retry is there, flag-service

39
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:04,441
isn't.

40
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:14,679
One flag arms it. The discriminator
becomes a native v2alpha1 alert with warn

41
00:03:14,759 --> 00:03:20,552
and critical thresholds. Replay the fault,
and at t plus one eighty-two seconds the

42
00:03:20,632 --> 00:03:25,184
webhook fires. Everyone else in this
category stops at a panel.

43
00:03:25,264 --> 00:03:31,057
None of them hand you back a query, a
dashboard, and an armed tripwire.

44
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:38,438
Run it twice. Same input, same seed, same
verdict hash.

45
00:03:38,518 --> 00:03:43,483
There is no LLM anywhere in the runtime —
this is deterministic.

46
00:03:43,563 --> 00:03:48,529
The corpus is synthetic and disclosed;
ground truth comes from a manifest.

47
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:53,852
Everyone can show you the difference. Only
SigNoz can arm it.

48
00:03:53,932 --> 00:03:56,414
One command reproduces the whole thing.
